<article class="article-layout">
    <header class="post-hero">
        <div class="post-hero-inner">
            <h1>Local-First, or: How I Learned to Stop Browsing and Love the Machine</h1>
            <time datetime="2026-05-04">04.05.2026</time>
        </div>
    </header>
    <div class="post-body">
        <p>I still remember the day we got our first computer. It was an Intel 486 with a tiny bit of RAM, I believe it to be 4MB. My dad used to work at the desk every night after coming back from the university, to prepare lessons, and documents, and to meddle with strange programs to do simple things. I would sit by his desk and would spend countless hours watching him, learning the commands, playing around with the terminal, even if we never did something remotely sophisticated. He used to take contrarian moves to everything, so instead of using Microsoft Word, he went the WordPerfect and LaTEX route, and would be preaching frequently on <em>how difficult things were with Microsoft Word</em>. Strangely enough, people around seemed not to bother much with it, taking a few workarounds here and there to achieve stuff using Word. They got used to the interface with its quirks and conditions, they got used to accepting things the way they were, to just concede and get the job done.</p>
<p>A few years afterwards, we bought a more capable machine, and I started coding out of necessity. I was using the now-extinct-but-absolutely-fantastic Flash to animate a movie, but just moving things around in frames didn't make sense anymore. I wanted some interaction, and I started coding on Actionscript (a javascript-inspired scripting language), and I built what I call &quot;games&quot;, but were in reality just a few interactive prototypes testing the limits of what coding without StackOverflow would feel like, at a time when I didn't have training on programming concepts or enough use cases/motivation/incentives to build. It was fun to draw some objects and animate them with code, and people loved it so much that they started using it for everything, which made it insecure. Flash was banned after a while by all browsers and <em>we moved on</em>.</p>
<figure>
  <picture>
    <source type="image/webp" srcset="/moui-grove/assets/QWOP_screenshot-480w.webp 480w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px">
